from-json "(Some(dash), Some(dot)) if dash < dot => {"
to-json "(Some(dash), Some(dot)) if dash <= dot => {"
why In normalise, both indices come from the same immutable file string via rfind of the distinct nonempty ASCII characters '-' and '.'. If both are Some, their match positions cannot be equal: one byte position cannot match both characters. Thus dash < dot and dash <= dot have identical truth values for every defined Rust string input here; all other Option cases, branch work, outputs and index progress stay unchanged. This is a source-context proof, not survival-based inference or a claim that arbitrary integer comparisons are interchangeable. Permanent controls compile this exact native edit beside the original with independent literal basenames for rlib/rmeta, paths, empty/missing/reversed/multiple punctuation and Unicode, plus a bounded 5461-string helper grid and unequal-index predicate observations. The finite grid supplements the universal distinct-search argument and is not an exhaustive-universe claim. A same-character search refutes that predicate premise only, without claiming a wider final-string counterexample; equal-index and wrong-negative oracles fail. Revalidate if search patterns/receivers, comparison/context, or relevant string contracts change. Canonical proof: docs/specs/trust/flag-normalization-domain.md (leaf M3.6.9.6.3.2.2.13.2.5).

